{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive level bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR, occasionally more). Apply a downward intercept shift to the entire path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the recent history rolls over from a peak, the model keeps prices elevated and declines too slowly. Increase sensitivity to negative momentum (steepen early-horizon declines).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: model tends to revert upward toward prior highs, while the market mean-reverts downward to a lower regime. Anchor forecasts to recent lows/short-term moving average rather than the 30d peak.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: bias grows with horizon (later days show larger overestimation). Use a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (e.g., linear or convex with day-ahead).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following high prints in history (80–84), the model projects rebounds/back-to-highs that do not materialize. Cap rebound magnitude and require confirmation (e.g., multiple up days) before forecasting a recovery.",
    "Volatility/dispersion too low around selloffs: realized paths show sharper step-downs; model paths are smoother. Add a ‘selloff jump’ heuristic when consecutive down days occur (or when price breaks below short MA), shifting the whole near-term path lower."
  ]
}
